  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms \npossible on Monday, heavy downpours, gusty winds, and hail \npossible.\n\nThe remainder of Sunday will remain dry and pleasant with fair \nweather cumulus clouds overhead. The forecast becomes tricky\nafter today though with multiple rounds of storms possible for\nmonday, but low confidence in the evolution and placement of the\nlarge scale forcing. \n\nAn MCS developing over Lake Michigan will slowly continue to\nprogress eastward through the rest of this afternoon and into\ntonight. This feature and how it evolves with time will play a\nkey role in thunderstorm development over central Pennsylvania\ntomorrow. The timing of the MCS in the morning as if moves over\nLake Erie along with multiple 850mb shortwaves will serve as \nthe catalysts for storm initiation. \n\nCurrent model solutions depict two main rounds of storms for \ntomorrow. One is in the morning along the outflow of the MCS.\nMorning CAPE in northwestern PA will be between 500-1000J/kg \nand available bulk windshear will be around 25kts. These storms\nwill favor a linear storm motion and could contain strong to\nsevere wind gusts as they propagate along the MCS cold pool.\nWhile the environment that early in the morning is not entirely\nfavorable for stronger storms, the possibility is still there. \nThe second round of convection would be most likely to initiate\nin the mid to late afternoon as a second shortwave comes across\nthe Allegheny Front. The environment at this time would more\nsupportive of severe storms if they can initiate. Widespread \nCAPE between 1500-2000J/kg is being depicted in the mean 12Z \nHREF solution along with area wide 30kts of bulk shear. This is\nthe solution with the highest confidence as of right now, but\nthe question will come down to where storms initiate and the\neastward extent of those storms. If the MCS cannot maintain its\ncurrent strength and speed overnight then Monday could look\nentirely different than what was just described. \n\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The unsettled pattern continues after Monday \nwith daily afternoon storms chances through mid week. \n\nAfter Monday we can expect continued daily afternoon chances for\nshowers and thunderstorms at least through Thursday. Repeated\nshort waves rotating around a larger upper level low, along \nwith moisture injections from the gulf, and daily highs in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s will provide ample instability and lift for\nafternoon storms. It is too early to say with any confidence \nwhether or not these storms will pose a significant severe \nthreat, as these will mostly be typical afternoon summer time \nstorms. However, the SPC has painted an area of 15%\nprobabilitiesfor severe weather on Wednesday that includes \nportions of southwestern PA. \n\nWhile severe weather confidence remains low for Tuesday through\nThursday, there is likely going to be a daily threat for\nisolated to scattered flash flooding. Model mean PWATs are\nbetween 1.5-2\" meaning these storms will likely be efficient \nrain producers, and given current antecedent conditions it \nwould not take a ton of rain to cause problems in areas that \nhave seen repeated flash flooding over the past week.",
